
---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Trees & Shrubs | Silken Valley Lifestyle"
  description="Explore deciduous trees, evergreen trees, flowering shrubs, hedges, landscape planting, and woody plants suited to the Okanagan."
  heroTitle="Trees & Shrubs"
  heroText="Discover the beauty, structure, and lasting character of trees and shrubs."
  heroCompact={true}
>
  <section class="tree-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Trees, Shrubs & Landscape Inspiration</p>
    <h1>The Foundation of a Beautiful Garden</h1>

    <p>
      Trees and shrubs form the framework of the landscape.
      They provide shade, shelter, privacy, seasonal colour,
      fragrance, and year-round structure. From majestic
      deciduous trees and elegant flowering shrubs to
      architectural evergreens and carefully clipped hedges,
      woody plants bring lasting character to outdoor spaces.
    </p>

    <p>
      Understanding their mature size, growing habits,
      seasonal interest, and environmental requirements
      helps create balanced gardens that remain beautiful
      and functional for many years.
    </p>
  </section>

  <nav class="tree-nav" aria-label="Trees and shrubs sections">
    <a href="#deciduous">Deciduous Trees</a>
    <a href="#evergreen-trees">Evergreen Trees</a>
    <a href="#flowering-shrubs">Flowering Shrubs</a>
    <a href="#evergreen-shrubs">Evergreen Shrubs</a>
    <a href="#hedges">Hedges & Screening</a>
    <a href="#planting">Planting & Care</a>
    <a href="#okanagan">Okanagan Plants</a>
  </nav>

  <section class="tree-section" id="deciduous">
    <p class="eyebrow">Seasonal Beauty & Shade</p>
    <h2>Deciduous Trees</h2>

    <p>
      Deciduous trees lose their leaves during autumn or
      the dormant season. They offer changing displays
      throughout the year, from spring blossoms and summer
      shade to brilliant autumn foliage and attractive
      winter silhouettes.
    </p>

    <p>
      When selecting a deciduous tree, consider its mature
      height and spread, root characteristics, growth rate,
      seasonal appearance, and suitability for the available
      space.
    </p>

    <div class="tree-grid">
      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Japanese Maple</h3>
        <p>
          Valued for delicate foliage, graceful branching,
          and exceptional seasonal colour. Japanese maples
          are often used as specimen trees in sheltered
          garden locations.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Flowering Crabapple</h3>
        <p>
          An ornamental tree producing beautiful spring
          blossoms, followed by small decorative fruits.
          Many cultivars also offer attractive autumn colour.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Magnolia</h3>
        <p>
          Magnolias are celebrated for their large, elegant
          flowers, which appear in spring or summer depending
          on the species. They make impressive ornamental
          specimen trees.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Birch</h3>
        <p>
          Recognized for graceful branching and distinctive
          bark, particularly in white-barked varieties.
          Birch trees bring lightness and texture to the
          landscape.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Ginkgo</h3>
        <p>
          An ancient tree species with distinctive fan-shaped
          leaves that turn brilliant yellow in autumn.
          Male cultivars are commonly selected for landscapes
          to avoid the unpleasant-smelling mature seeds
          produced by female trees.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Maples</h3>
        <p>
          A diverse group of trees offering shade, attractive
          foliage, and seasonal colour. Different species
          vary considerably in mature size and site requirements.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Ornamental Cherry</h3>
        <p>
          Known for spectacular spring flowering, ornamental
          cherries create beautiful seasonal displays and
          are often planted as focal points or along pathways.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Oak</h3>
        <p>
          Long-lived trees with strong branching structure
          and impressive presence. Oaks can provide valuable
          shade and habitat where sufficient space is available.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tree-section" id="evergreen-trees">
    <p class="eyebrow">Structure Through Every Season</p>
    <h2>Evergreen Trees</h2>

    <p>
      Evergreen trees retain their foliage throughout the year,
      providing continuity, shelter, privacy, and structure
      during the winter months. They are especially useful
      for establishing the permanent framework of a garden.
    </p>

    <p>
      Conifers are an important group of evergreen trees,
      ranging from tall architectural specimens to compact
      varieties suitable for smaller gardens.
    </p>

    <div class="tree-grid">
      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Blue Spruce</h3>
        <p>
          Recognized for its blue-grey needles and distinctive
          pyramidal form. Blue spruce makes a striking specimen
          tree where there is room for its mature size.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Douglas Fir</h3>
        <p>
          A large evergreen conifer with a strong upright
          habit and soft green needles. It is best suited
          to landscapes with generous space.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Scots Pine</h3>
        <p>
          A distinctive pine with attractive orange-brown
          bark on mature trunks and an open, irregular crown.
          It provides character and year-round structure.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Columnar Juniper</h3>
        <p>
          Narrow, upright evergreens useful for vertical
          accents, screening, and formal garden compositions.
          Many varieties tolerate sunny and relatively dry sites.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Western Red Cedar</h3>
        <p>
          A tall evergreen with dense, scale-like foliage.
          It is commonly used for privacy planting and
          screening in suitable growing conditions.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>European Larch</h3>
        <p>
          An unusual deciduous conifer that produces soft
          green needles in spring and summer before turning
          golden in autumn and dropping for winter.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tree-section" id="flowering-shrubs">
    <p class="eyebrow">Flowers, Fragrance & Seasonal Interest</p>
    <h2>Flowering Shrubs</h2>

    <p>
      Flowering shrubs bring colour, fragrance, and structure
      to garden borders. They can be planted individually
      as focal points, grouped into informal shrub borders,
      or incorporated into mixed perennial gardens.
    </p>

    <p>
      Choosing shrubs with different flowering periods
      helps create a sequence of seasonal interest from
      early spring through autumn.
    </p>

    <div class="tree-grid">
      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Lilac</h3>
        <p>
          A traditional garden favourite known for fragrant
          spring flower clusters. Lilacs are available in
          shades of purple, pink, blue, and white.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Hydrangea</h3>
        <p>
          Popular for generous flower heads and attractive
          foliage. Different species and cultivars have
          varying preferences for sunlight, moisture,
          and pruning.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Mock Orange</h3>
        <p>
          A fragrant flowering shrub producing white
          blossoms in late spring or early summer.
          It is a lovely addition to traditional gardens.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Spirea</h3>
        <p>
          A versatile group of shrubs offering clusters
          of flowers and attractive foliage. Different
          varieties provide spring or summer interest.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Forsythia</h3>
        <p>
          Known for brilliant yellow flowers that appear
          on bare branches in early spring, bringing
          an early burst of colour to the landscape.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Weigela</h3>
        <p>
          A flowering shrub with trumpet-shaped blooms
          in shades of pink, red, and white. Many cultivars
          also offer attractive foliage colour.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Potentilla</h3>
        <p>
          A hardy, generally low-growing shrub that produces
          flowers over a long summer period. It is useful
          for sunny borders and informal plantings.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Viburnum</h3>
        <p>
          A diverse group of shrubs with ornamental flowers,
          attractive foliage, and, in some species,
          decorative berries and autumn colour.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tree-section" id="evergreen-shrubs">
    <p class="eyebrow">Permanent Form & Foliage</p>
    <h2>Evergreen Shrubs</h2>

    <p>
      Evergreen shrubs maintain foliage throughout the year
      and provide a dependable foundation for garden design.
      They can be used for structure, low hedging, screening,
      groundcover, and formal planting arrangements.
    </p>

    <div class="tree-grid">
      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Boxwood</h3>
        <p>
          Traditionally used in formal gardens, parterres,
          and clipped hedges. Its small evergreen leaves
          and tolerance of shaping make it useful for
          geometric planting designs.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Holly</h3>
        <p>
          Valued for glossy foliage and, on female plants
          with suitable pollination, colourful berries.
          Holly provides winter interest and evergreen structure.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Yew</h3>
        <p>
          A classic evergreen used in formal landscapes,
          topiary, and clipped hedges. Yew tolerates pruning
          well but is toxic if ingested by people or animals.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Juniper</h3>
        <p>
          Available in upright, spreading, and groundcover
          forms. Junipers are useful for sunny locations
          and many varieties tolerate dry conditions once
          established.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Rhododendron</h3>
        <p>
          Evergreen shrubs with large, impressive flower
          clusters. They generally prefer acidic, organic,
          well-drained soil and protection from harsh conditions.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Mountain Laurel</h3>
        <p>
          An ornamental evergreen shrub with attractive
          foliage and distinctive spring flowers.
          It prefers acidic soil and suitable moisture conditions.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tree-section" id="hedges">
    <p class="eyebrow">Boundaries, Privacy & Garden Rooms</p>
    <h2>Hedges & Screening</h2>

    <p>
      Hedges provide boundaries, privacy, shelter, and
      definition within the landscape. They can separate
      garden rooms, frame entrances, create pathways,
      or form the backdrop for flower borders.
    </p>

    <div class="tree-grid">
      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Formal Hedges</h3>
        <p>
          Carefully clipped hedges create strong geometric
          lines and a sense of order. Boxwood, yew, and
          suitable other evergreen shrubs are traditional choices.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Informal Hedges</h3>
        <p>
          Flowering shrubs and mixed plantings create
          softer boundaries while providing seasonal
          flowers, foliage, and wildlife habitat.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Privacy Screening</h3>
        <p>
          Carefully selected trees and shrubs can screen
          neighbouring properties, roads, and unwanted
          views. Consider mature height, width, and
          spacing before planting.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Windbreaks & Shelterbelts</h3>
        <p>
          Strategically positioned trees and shrubs can
          reduce wind exposure and provide shelter for
          garden spaces and more delicate plants.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tree-section" id="planting">
    <p class="eyebrow">Establishing Healthy Woody Plants</p>
    <h2>Planting & Maintaining Trees and Shrubs</h2>

    <p>
      Correct planting and early care are essential for
      establishing healthy trees and shrubs. Before planting,
      consider the plant's mature dimensions, soil conditions,
      drainage, sunlight, and available growing space.
    </p>

    <ul class="tree-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Site selection:</strong>
        Match each plant to the available sunlight, exposure,
        soil, and drainage conditions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Planting depth:</strong>
        Position the root flare of trees at or slightly above
        the finished soil level. Avoid burying the trunk.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Root preparation:</strong>
        Carefully loosen circling or tightly bound roots
        where appropriate before planting.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Watering:</strong>
        Water thoroughly after planting and maintain
        consistent moisture while roots establish.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mulching:</strong>
        Apply organic mulch to help conserve moisture
        and moderate soil temperatures, keeping it away
        from direct contact with trunks and stems.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pruning:</strong>
        Remove damaged or diseased growth and prune
        according to the species and its flowering habits.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Spacing:</strong>
        Allow sufficient room for the mature height,
        spread, and root development of each plant.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="tree-section" id="okanagan">
    <p class="eyebrow">Regional Plant Selection</p>
    <h2>Trees & Shrubs for the Okanagan</h2>

    <p>
      The Okanagan's climate offers excellent opportunities
      for growing a diverse range of trees and shrubs.
      However, hot, dry summers, limited summer rainfall,
      varying soil conditions, and winter temperature
      fluctuations make careful plant selection important.
    </p>

    <p>
      Select plants according to their hardiness, mature size,
      water requirements, and tolerance of local growing
      conditions. Newly planted trees and shrubs need
      particular attention to irrigation while their root
      systems become established.
    </p>

    <div class="tree-grid">
      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Drought-Conscious Planting</h3>
        <p>
          Consider suitable junipers, selected pines,
          drought-tolerant shrubs, and plants adapted
          to sunny, well-drained locations.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Shade Trees</h3>
        <p>
          Choose trees with appropriate mature dimensions
          to provide shade for patios, outdoor living spaces,
          and garden planting areas.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Flowering Trees & Shrubs</h3>
        <p>
          Incorporate suitable flowering crabapples,
          lilacs, spireas, and other locally hardy plants
          for seasonal colour.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="tree-card">
        <h3>Evergreen Structure</h3>
        <p>
          Use appropriate evergreen trees and shrubs
          to provide privacy, shelter, and winter interest
          in the landscape.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tree-closing">
    <p class="eyebrow">The Lasting Beauty of the Landscape</p>
    <h2>Planting for the Future</h2>
    <p>
      Trees and shrubs are long-term investments in the
      garden. With thoughtful selection, appropriate
      placement, and proper care, they create beautiful
      outdoor spaces that mature and develop character
      over many years.
    </p>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .tree-intro {
    max-width: 850px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .tree-intro > p:not(.eyebrow) {
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  .tree-nav {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: .65rem;
    margin: 0 auto 2.5rem;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 12px;
  }

  .tree-nav a {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: .5rem .9rem;
    background: var(--rose-wash);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 20px;
    color: var(--accent-dark);
    font-size: .9rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .tree-nav a:hover {
    background: var(--accent);
    color: #ffffff;
  }

  .tree-section {
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 14px;
    box-shadow: 0 3px 12px rgba(90, 50, 65, .04);
    scroll-margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .tree-section h2,
  .tree-closing h2 {
    color: var(--accent-dark);
    margin-top: .25rem;
  }

  .tree-section > p:not(.eyebrow) {
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  .tree-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .tree-card {
    padding: 1.2rem;
    background: var(--rose-wash);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
  }

  .tree-card h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
    color: var(--accent-dark);
  }

  .tree-card p {
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 0;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .tree-list {
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.9;
  }

  .tree-list li {
    margin-bottom: .65rem;
  }

  .tree-closing {
    margin: 2rem 0 1rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    text-align: center;
    background: var(--rose-wash);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 14px;
  }

  .tree-closing p:last-child {
    max-width: 700px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .tree-section {
      padding: 1.25rem;
    }

    .tree-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .tree-nav {
      gap: .5rem;
      padding: 1rem;
    }

    .tree-nav a {
      font-size: .85rem;
    }

    .tree-closing {
      padding: 1.5rem;
    }
  }
</style>
